WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.530
Imagine you've been given a $180 million
bonus at workWould you call it a career and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.530 --> 00:00:15.120
retire in paradise or would you dare to bet it&nbsp;
all away on a new venture to try to change the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:24.780
world? That is the choice Elon Musk faced and&nbsp;
he chose to risk it all. It's hard to find a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:31.140
company that generates as much excitement about&nbsp;
the future than SpaceX does today. In May 2020,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.140 --> 00:00:36.930
it launched astronauts into orbit - the first time&nbsp;
that happened from American soil in nine years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:45.060
Yet in the not so distant past, SpaceX nearly went&nbsp;
bankrupt at the same time Tesla was struggling to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:51.690
survive. I talked about that in my last video.&nbsp;
Elon had to try to save two companies at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.690 --> 00:01:03.150
same time. This is the story of how SpaceX nearly&nbsp;
failed. When the company Elon co-founded x.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:09.750
merged with a rival to become PayPal which was&nbsp;
then acquired by eBay in 2002, Elon walked away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:16.800
with $180 million. He poured all of his winnings&nbsp;
into new companies because he wanted to work on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:23.520
the problems that he thought would most affect the&nbsp;
future of humanity. He put $70 million into Tesla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:30.180
$10 million into SolarCity and $100 million&nbsp;
into SpaceX. He had to borrow money for rent.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:37.510
Elon went to Russia to figure out how much a&nbsp;
rocket launch would cost. He planned on buying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:43.480
a refurbished intercontinental ballistic missile,&nbsp;
like one of these. He had a bizarre plan to send a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:49.870
greenhouse to Mars so plants could grow on the Red&nbsp;
Planet. The idea was to interest people in space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:56.350
and hopefully increase NASA's budget. He wanted&nbsp;
to purchase a rocket for $4 million but the lowest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.350 --> 00:02:02.230
price the Russians gave him was $8 million.&nbsp;
The sale didn't go through. He realized on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:08.920
the flight home that the cost of building a rocket&nbsp;
was only 3% of the sales price so if he could make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:15.190
the rockets himself it would drastically bring&nbsp;
down the cost. And that is how SpaceX began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:21.940
by building its own rockets. Well, actually,&nbsp;
SpaceX began with a carpet and a mariachi band&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:29.230
but ended up building a dream. In many ways,&nbsp;
this dream was insane. Elon didn't have any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.230 --> 00:02:33.910
experience in the rocket business. He had never&nbsp;
built anything like that before and had to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:40.780
books about it to learn more. He knew it was going&nbsp;
to be tough and thought SpaceX would fail but he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:45.970
once said in an interview that when something&nbsp;
is important enough you do it even when the odds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.970 --> 00:02:52.810
are not in your favor. And he thought this was&nbsp;
important enough. He believed reducing the cost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.810 --> 00:02:58.690
of spaceflight was essential to the survival of&nbsp;
humanity in case there's a cataclysmic event that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:06.100
wipes out life on Earth. His vision is for humans&nbsp;
to live on other planets including Mars. If we are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:12.430
able to increase the rate of innovation then life&nbsp;
can become multi-planetary. This is the goal we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.430 --> 00:03:17.170
should strive for. He went about recruiting some&nbsp;
of the brightest engineers in America for his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.170 --> 00:03:22.120
new company. We're looking for great people of all&nbsp;
kinds to join the company so if you're interested,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:27.940
please send us your resume. They worked non-stop&nbsp;
and designed almost every part of the rocket.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.940 --> 00:03:34.120
The first rocket they built was the Falcon 1.&nbsp;
Elon had an ambitious timeline of launching the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.120 --> 00:03:41.680
rocket in November 2003 - just 18 months after&nbsp;
he founded SpaceX. That didn't happen. Falcon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:49.300
1's first attempt to launch would not be until&nbsp;
March 2006. It was a failure. It only spent 41&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:57.670
seconds in the air before crashing down violently&nbsp;
because of a fuel leak. The following year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.670 --> 00:04:03.220
in March 2007, the second launch attempt went&nbsp;
better and everyone thought that they had made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:09.640
it. Stages are separated. Stage separation&nbsp;
happens when the lower portion of the rocket,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:15.070
called the first stage or booster, separates&nbsp;
from the rest of the rocket once the propellant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:20.860
which is what feeds the engine is used up. But&nbsp;
things didn't go as planned. The rocket started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:26.950
to wiggle and the vibrations caused the fuel to&nbsp;
slosh around. The slosh created an opening in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:35.630
engine. When the engines sucked in air, the rocket&nbsp;
flamed out. In August 2008, the Falcon 1 made its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:41.990
third attempt. Again, it went well at the start&nbsp;
but then the first and second stages collided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:49.340
after they separated. SpaceX had three failed&nbsp;
rocket launches. Three strikes, it was almost out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:55.310
To make matters worse, the 2008 financial crisis&nbsp;
made it difficult to get funding from investors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.310 --> 00:05:02.000
and at the same time, Elon had to scramble to&nbsp;
save Tesla which was also about to die. Plus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.140
he was going through a divorce from his wife&nbsp;
Justine. Elon set 2008 was the worst year of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:15.410
life and he was close to a nervous breakdown. He&nbsp;
later said this reflecting on the difficult time:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:22.760
"I don't ever give up. I'd have to be dead or&nbsp;
completely incapacitated." The future of SpaceX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.760 --> 00:05:30.410
rested on the fourth launch. If the Falcon&nbsp;
1 failed to reach orbit again it would have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:39.292
been game over for the company. We have lift&nbsp;
off. On September 28, 2008,&nbsp;success, at last.

00:05:39.292 --> 00:05:45.260
Stage separation confirmed. It&nbsp;
finally reached orbit. But even

00:05:45.260 --> 00:05:49.820
with a successful launch, the company was&nbsp;
still in trouble. It didn't have any money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:57.530
left. Elon had to figure out what to do. But then&nbsp;
a phone call on December 23rd changed everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.530 --> 00:06:04.370
NASA had taken note of the successful&nbsp;
launch and awarded SpaceX a $1.6 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.370 --> 00:06:10.100
contract to resupply the International Space&nbsp;
Station. And the next day, on Christmas Eve,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.100 --> 00:06:16.460
Tesla secured a round of financing that saved&nbsp;
the company. Elon came so close to losing both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:23.150
companies at the end of 2008 yet at the very last&nbsp;
minute, they both pulled through. But this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:28.700
still an uphill battle. Even his heroes like Neil&nbsp;
Armstrong, the first person to walk on the moon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:34.580
appeared to be against him. Armstrong was worried&nbsp;
that the Obama administration's reliance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:40.640
private companies could mean the U.S. and NASA&nbsp;
would lose its dominance in space because they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.640 --> 00:06:46.640
would have no means of getting there themselves.&nbsp;
After the success of the Falcon 1's fourth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.640 --> 00:06:52.370
attempt and the subsequent contract with NASA,
SpaceX turned its attention to building a bigger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:59.060
rocket, the Falcon 9. You can actually, literally&nbsp;
fit a city bus in that fairing - it's that big.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.060 --> 00:07:04.730
The Falcon 9 would eventually carry a Dragon&nbsp;
capsule to the International Space Station. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:10.280
capsule could be used for future flights with&nbsp;
crew on board as NASA had retired its space&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:15.950
shuttle fleet in 2011 and had been relying on the&nbsp;
Russians to send American astronauts into space.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.950 --> 00:07:23.750
There was just one problem with the Falcon 9: it&nbsp;
was not reusable at the time. Elon knew building&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:29.600
reusable rockets was the only way to make space&nbsp;
travel affordable. It would allow for about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.600 --> 00:07:35.000
hundred-fold reduction in launch costs. You&nbsp;
can imagine that if planes were not reusable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:41.870
very few people would fly. SpaceX worked on&nbsp;
modifying the Falcon 9 to preserve the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.870 --> 00:07:46.850
stage, the booster, the most expensive part.&nbsp;
To give you an idea of how difficult it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.850 --> 00:07:52.250
to land that part back on Earth, picture a&nbsp;
pencil breaking apart and the lower portion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.250 --> 00:08:01.850
somehow lands right-side-up and intact. It's&nbsp;
tough to do. SpaceX failed over and over and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.850 --> 00:08:11.540
over again but then on December 22, 2015, the&nbsp;
Falcon 9 landed its booster for the first time.

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:24.770
SpaceX employees were more than a little&nbsp;
excited. And then in 2018, SpaceX built a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.770 --> 00:08:35.720
more powerful rocket, the Falcon Heavy which is&nbsp;
made up of three Falcon nines. Two out of those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.720 --> 00:08:41.570
three boosters managed to land although the third,&nbsp;
the core booster, crashed into the ocean rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.570 --> 00:08:47.450
than land on a drone ship but the mission was a&nbsp;
success because the Falcon Heavy launched a Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:54.680
Roadster carrying a dummy astronaut into orbit.&nbsp;
Two years later, SpaceX would send real astronauts

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:55.310
to space. Liftoff of the Falcon 9 and Crew&nbsp;
Dragon. Go NASA, go SpaceX, Godspeed Bob and Doug.

00:08:55.310 --> 00:09:08.930
In May 2020, NASA astronauts Bob Behnken&nbsp;
and Doug Hurley traveled to the ISS on&nbsp;

00:09:08.930 --> 00:09:17.870
board the Crew Dragon spacecraft carried by the&nbsp;
Falcon 9. It was a historic moment - the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.870 --> 00:09:23.180
time a private company had put humans into&nbsp;
orbit. This is a dream come true, I think,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.180 --> 00:09:28.940
for me and everyone at SpaceX. This is not&nbsp;
something that I ever thought would actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:35.030
happen And now SpaceX is looking ahead to even&nbsp;
more ambitious plans. It's setting its sights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:41.090
on developing a fully reusable rocket called&nbsp;
Starship to carry people to the moon, Mars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.090 --> 00:09:47.480
and back. According to the company, a round-trip&nbsp;
ticket to Mars could cost between $100,000 to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.480 --> 00:09:53.660
$500,000. The idea is that it would be possible&nbsp;
for people in developed countries to sell their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:59.660
homes and move to the Red Planet if they want&nbsp;
to. But all of these plans nearly never happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.660 --> 00:10:06.830
because SpaceX nearly died. If it weren't for the&nbsp;
success of that fourth launch attempt in 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.830 --> 00:10:14.150
SpaceX would have run out of money and shut down.&nbsp;
Death was all but guaranteed. But it managed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.150 --> 00:10:21.050
stay alive by the skin of its teeth and Tesla&nbsp;
did the same. If there's any lesson to take away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.050 --> 00:10:28.100
it's to never, ever give up. I hope enjoyed&nbsp;
what you saw. If you did, give us a like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

